p = mk_pack("form4-history", "History — Form 4", "Form 4", "History", "history",
    "Fourth-year History, leading into Form 5: African nationalism after 1945, nationalism and political parties in Cameroon, independence in 1960, the UN plebiscites and reunification of 1961, the Federal Republic and the unitary state, international organisations, and the Cold War in outline.",
    "Cameroon anglophone secondary History syllabus, Form 4 — to be checked against the official text", HIS_NOTE)

# ------------------------------------------------------------ CH1 nationalism
c = p.chapter("nationalism", "African nationalism and Cameroon")
L = LS(c, "nationalism-africa", "African nationalism after 1945",
    ["Define nationalism and decolonisation", "Explain the causes of the growth of African nationalism", "Describe the methods of nationalists", "Name key events in the road to independence"],
    [("text", "**Nationalism** is the desire of a people to rule themselves and to be free of foreign control. **Decolonisation** is the process by which colonies became independent. After 1945 African nationalism grew quickly. Many Africans wanted **political freedom, equality, better education, fair pay** and an end to forced labour, taxes and racial discrimination."),
     ("key", "retenir", "Causes", "- **The Second World War**: African soldiers saw colonial powers weakened and had been told the war was for freedom.\n- **Education**: schools produced educated Africans who read about freedom and rights.\n- **Ideas**: the Atlantic Charter, the UN Charter and **Pan-Africanism** (the 1945 Pan-African Congress in Manchester).\n- **Examples abroad**: India and other Asian countries gained independence in the late 1940s.\n- **Economic hardship** and dislike of colonial taxes and forced labour."),
     ("key", "retenir", "Methods and events", "Nationalists formed **political parties** and **trade unions**, held meetings, wrote in **newspapers**, organised **strikes and boycotts**, petitioned governments and took part in **elections**. In some places there were armed struggles, in others peaceful negotiations. **Ghana** (the Gold Coast) became independent in **1957**, the first black African colony in the south of the Sahara to do so; **1960** is called the **'Year of Africa'** because many colonies became independent."),
     ("text", "Colonial powers also changed: after the war Britain and France gave Africans some **political rights**, such as places in councils and assemblies, but at first these were limited. Colonial powers were also costly to maintain and under pressure from the UN and from public opinion. By the mid-1960s most of Africa had become independent, although some colonies fought long wars or waited until later."),
     ("key", "pieges", "Common mistakes", "- Nationalism did not begin suddenly in 1945; it built on earlier protests and resistance, but it grew faster after the war.\n- Independence was not always won by war: many countries gained it by negotiation.\n- 'Year of Africa' refers to 1960, not 1957.")],
    [("Example 1: causes", "Give two reasons why the Second World War encouraged nationalism in Africa.", ["African soldiers served abroad and saw that colonial powers could be defeated.", "The Allies spoke of freedom and self-determination.", "These ideas encouraged demands for independence."], "Soldiers' experience and the language of freedom."),
     ("Example 2: methods", "Name two peaceful methods nationalists used.", ["They founded parties and unions.", "They held meetings, wrote newspapers and took part in elections.", "Strikes and petitions were also used."], "Parties/elections and newspapers/strikes.")],
    F(tlfig(1945, 1965, [(1945, "1945"), (1957, "1957"), (1960, "1960"), (1963, "1963")], h=200), "Road to independence", "A timeline from 1945 to 1965 with 1945, 1957, 1960 and 1963."),
    [MCQ("In Form 4 history, the first sub-Saharan colony in Africa to gain independence after 1945 was", "Ghana (Gold Coast) in 1957", ["Nigeria in 1960", "Cameroon in 1972", "Egypt in 1884"], "Ghana became independent in 1957."),
     TF("1960 is often called the Year of Africa.", True, "Many African states became independent that year."),
     MATCH("Match each cause with its explanation.", [("Education", "Produced Africans who demanded rights"), ("World War II", "Weakened colonial powers and encouraged hopes"), ("Pan-Africanism", "Idea of African unity and freedom")], "Causes of nationalism."),
     MCQ("Which of these was a method of nationalists?", "forming political parties", ["closing all schools", "joining the colonial army only", "ignoring the law"], "Parties organised the movement.", tier="approfondissement"),
     OPEN("Explain four causes of the growth of African nationalism after 1945.", "African soldiers' war experience; the weakening of Britain and France; education and new ideas such as Pan-Africanism and the Atlantic Charter; example of India's independence; hardships under colonial rule.", "1 mark per cause with explanation.", points=4, tier="approfondissement"),
     PROB("A student writes about decolonisation.",
          [("mcq", "Decolonisation means", "the end of colonial rule", ["the start of colonial rule", "a farming method", "a trade fair"], "Colonies became independent.", {}),
           ("num", "In what year did Ghana become independent?", 1957, "Ghana became independent in 1957.", 0, None, {}),
           ("tf", "All African countries became independent through war.", False, "Many used negotiation.", {}),
           ("open", "Give two things nationalist newspapers did.", "They spread nationalist ideas, criticised colonial policy, informed people of their rights and organised protests.", "1 mark per point.", {})])],
    [("The desire of a people to govern themselves is", "nationalism", ["colonialism", "plantation farming", "monarchy"], "Nationalism leads to demands for independence."),
     ("Pan-Africanism stresses", "African unity and freedom", ["European rule", "no cooperation", "isolation"], "It was an important idea."),
     ("Ghana's independence year was", "1957", ["1884", "1914", "1972"], "It was the first in sub-Saharan Africa."),
     ("The Year of Africa is", "1960", ["1945", "1957", "1990"], "Many independences occurred in 1960."),
     ("A boycott is", "a refusal to buy or use something as protest", ["a kind of tax", "a type of school", "a form of music"], "It is a method of protest.")],
    ["Teacher to verify: Ghana's independence date (1957), the 1945 Manchester Congress, and the wording 'first in sub-Saharan Africa' (strictly, Ghana was the first British colony in black Africa to gain independence)."], tag="Form 4 history nationalism")

L = LS(c, "nationalism-cameroon", "Nationalism and political parties in the Cameroons",
    ["Describe the growth of nationalism in French Cameroun", "Describe political developments in British Southern Cameroons", "Name the main parties and organisations", "Explain the role of the United Nations"],
    [("text", "In **French Cameroun** the main nationalist party was the **Union des Populations du Cameroun (UPC)**, founded in **1948**, which called for **independence and reunification** of the two Cameroons. Other parties and groups also formed as political life opened up after 1945. The UPC was banned in **1955** after unrest, and its leaders went underground or abroad, which led to a period of violence and conflict in some areas before and after independence."),
     ("key", "retenir", "British Cameroons", "In the **British Southern Cameroons**, early organisations included the **Cameroons Youth League** (from 1940) and later political parties: the **Kamerun National Congress (KNC)**, the **Kamerun People's Party (KPP)** and the **Kamerun National Democratic Party (KNDP)**, with the **Cameroon Peoples National Convention (CPNC)** later. Their leaders argued about whether to join Nigeria, join French Cameroun, or have another solution."),
     ("text", "In **1954** the Southern Cameroons obtained a **separate regional government** and a **House of Assembly** at Buea, with quasi-federal status in Nigeria. Elections to this house, held in the 1950s, were important steps. **Northern Cameroons** remained administered with Northern Nigeria. The **United Nations**, as supervisor of the trusteeship, sent visiting missions, discussed petitions and in the end arranged the plebiscites."),
     ("key", "attention", "Be careful", "- The two Cameroons followed different political paths because of different colonial administrations.\n- Historians and Cameroonians hold different views about these events; present them neutrally and use evidence.\n- Do not mix up party names: UPC (French Cameroun) versus KNC, KPP, KNDP and CPNC (British Cameroons).")],
    [("Example 1: parties", "Name the nationalist party founded in French Cameroun in 1948 and one party from the Southern Cameroons.", ["The UPC was founded in 1948.", "Southern Cameroons parties included the KNC and the KNDP.", "So UPC and, for example, KNDP."], "UPC; and for example KNC or KNDP."),
     ("Example 2: the role of the UN", "How did the UN supervise the trust territories?", ["It received reports from Britain and France.", "It sent visiting missions to the territories.", "It also considered petitions and arranged the plebiscites."], "By reports, visiting missions, petitions and plebiscites.")],
    F(cols("Parties in the two Cameroons", [("French Cameroun", ["UPC (1948)", "Other parties"]), ("Southern Cameroons", ["KNC", "KPP", "KNDP", "CPNC"])], w=420), "Some political parties", "Two columns naming parties in French Cameroun and in the Southern Cameroons."),
     [MCQ("The UPC was founded in", "1948", ["1884", "1916", "1972"], "It was founded in 1948."),
     TF("The Southern Cameroons obtained a separate regional government in 1954.", True, "The House of Assembly was at Buea."),
     MATCH("Match each party with its description.", [("UPC", "Nationalist party of French Cameroun"), ("KNC", "Southern Cameroons party of the early 1950s"), ("KNDP", "Southern Cameroons party formed in the mid-1950s")], "Parties and areas."),
     MCQ("Which body supervised the trust territories?", "the United Nations", ["the League of Nations", "the German Reichstag", "no one"], "The UN Trusteeship Council supervised them.", tier="approfondissement"),
     OPEN("Explain why the Southern and French Cameroons followed different political paths in the 1950s.", "They had been governed by Britain and France with different systems, languages and institutions for decades; the Southern Cameroons was attached to Nigeria and had its own House of Assembly, while French Cameroun had its own parties and administration.", "1 mark for different rulers; 1 mark for different institutions; 1 mark for results.", points=3, tier="approfondissement"),
     PROB("A student draws up a chart of political parties.",
          [("mcq", "Which party was banned in 1955?", "UPC", ["KNC", "KNDP", "CPNC"], "The UPC was banned in French Cameroun.", {}),
           ("tf", "The Southern Cameroons House of Assembly sat at Buea.", True, "Buea was its centre.", {}),
           ("mcq", "The body that arranged the plebiscites was the", "United Nations", ["World Bank", "OAU", "League of Nations"], "The UN organised them.", {}),
           ("open", "Give two methods political parties used to win support.", "Public meetings, newspapers and pamphlets, campaigning in elections, and organising supporters and youth groups.", "1 mark per method.", {})])],
    [("The UPC aimed at", "independence and reunification", ["permanent colonial rule", "union with Germany", "closing schools"], "These were its goals."),
     ("The KNC was a party in the", "Southern Cameroons", ["Sahara", "Lagos only", "Far North"], "It was active in the British zone."),
     ("The Cameroons Youth League began in", "1940", ["1884", "1957", "1972"], "It was an early association."),
     ("Buea became the seat of the", "Southern Cameroons House of Assembly", ["UN General Assembly", "French Parliament", "German Reichstag"], "It was the regional capital."),
     ("A plebiscite is", "a direct vote of the people on a single question", ["a type of tax", "a trade agreement", "a school test"], "It was used to decide the future of the British Cameroons.")],
    ["Teacher to verify: party names and founding dates (exact years of the KNC, KPP, KNDP and CPNC are deliberately vague; UPC 1948; ban in 1955; Cameroons Youth League 1940; KNC, KPP, KNDP, CPNC), the 1954 regional status, and the neutral wording about contested events; no individual politicians are named on purpose."], tag="Form 4 history Cameroons parties")

# ------------------------------------------------------------ CH2 independence & reunification
c = p.chapter("independence", "Independence and reunification")
L = LS(c, "independence-1960", "Independence of French Cameroun and Nigeria in 1960",
    ["Describe the independence of French Cameroun on 1 January 1960", "Describe the independence of Nigeria in October 1960", "Explain the effect on the British Cameroons"],
    [("text", "French Cameroun became independent on **1 January 1960** as the **Republic of Cameroon**, with Yaoundé as capital. This followed years of negotiation, political change within the French system and the work of the UN, which had ended the trusteeship for the French territory. The new state adopted a constitution and elected its first government."),
     ("key", "retenir", "Nigeria", "**Nigeria** became independent from Britain on **1 October 1960**. This was important for the British Cameroons because they had been governed as part of Nigeria. The UN now had to decide their future: remaining attached to an independent Nigeria was one possibility, and joining the independent Republic of Cameroon was another."),
     ("text", "In **1959** the UN General Assembly decided that a **plebiscite** (a vote of the people) should be held in the British Cameroons to ask them about their future. Both **Southern** and **Northern** Cameroons would vote separately. The question offered two choices: to **join the independent Republic of Nigeria** or to **join the independent Republic of Cameroon**. Some political groups wanted other options, but these were not in the vote."),
     ("key", "attention", "Be careful", "- Do not say the whole of Cameroon became independent in 1960: only French Cameroun did; the British Cameroons joined later (1961).\n- The plebiscite offered two options, joining Nigeria or joining Cameroon; people disagree about this arrangement, and historians discuss it.\n- Give dates exactly: 1 January 1960 and 1 October 1960."),
     ("key", "pieges", "Common mistakes", "- Independence of French Cameroun (1 January 1960) is not the same as reunification (1 October 1961).\n- Nigeria's independence date (1 October 1960) is a year before reunification, on the same day and month.\n- The UN, not Britain or France alone, decided how the British Cameroons would vote.")],
    [("Example 1: dates", "State the date of French Cameroun's independence and of Nigeria's independence.", ["French Cameroun: 1 January 1960.", "Nigeria: 1 October 1960.", "Both are in 1960 but nine months apart."], "1 January 1960 and 1 October 1960."),
     ("Example 2: the plebiscite choices", "Which two choices were offered to the British Cameroons in the plebiscite?", ["There were two options on the ballot.", "One was to join Nigeria.", "The other was to join the Republic of Cameroon."], "To join Nigeria or to join the Republic of Cameroon.")],
    F(tlfig(1958, 1962, [(1959, "1959"), (1960, "1960"), (1961, "1961")], h=200), "Years of change", "A timeline from 1958 to 1962 with 1959, 1960 and 1961."),
    [MCQ("French Cameroun became independent on", "1 January 1960", ["1 October 1960", "11 February 1961", "20 May 1972"], "That is the date."),
     TF("Nigeria became independent on 1 October 1960.", True, "That is the date."),
     MATCH("Match each event with its date.", [("Independence of French Cameroun", "1 January 1960"), ("Independence of Nigeria", "1 October 1960"), ("UN decision on a plebiscite", "1959")], "Dates around independence."),
     MCQ("The plebiscite in the British Cameroons was meant to decide", "whether to join Nigeria or the Republic of Cameroon", ["the price of cocoa", "the date of school", "the choice of a flag only"], "It was about their political future.", tier="approfondissement"),
     OPEN("Explain why Nigeria's independence made the future of the British Cameroons an urgent question.", "The British Cameroons were administered as part of Nigeria; once Nigeria became independent Britain would leave, so the UN had to decide whether the territories would join Nigeria or the Republic of Cameroon.", "1 mark each for administration with Nigeria, end of British rule and UN decision.", points=3, tier="approfondissement"),
     PROB("A class prepares a time chart for 1959–1961.",
          [("num", "In which year did the Republic of Cameroon become independent?", 1960, "It became independent on 1 January 1960.", 0, None, {}),
           ("mcq", "Which body decided that a plebiscite should be held?", "the United Nations General Assembly", ["the Commonwealth", "the OAU", "a school council"], "The UN decided in 1959.", {}),
           ("tf", "The British Cameroons became independent on 1 January 1960.", False, "Only French Cameroun did.", {}),
           ("open", "State two possible reasons why some people disagreed with the plebiscite options.", "Some wanted independence as a separate state; some thought other options like a federation should have been offered; others preferred a different timing.", "1 mark per reason.", {})])],
    [("The capital of the Republic of Cameroon in 1960 was", "Yaoundé", ["Buea", "Lagos", "Douala"], "Yaoundé became the capital."),
     ("Nigeria became independent in", "1960", ["1884", "1914", "1972"], "On 1 October 1960."),
     ("A plebiscite is", "a vote by the people on a main question", ["a tax", "a currency", "a treaty"], "It was used to decide the future."),
     ("The UN General Assembly decided in 1959 to organise", "plebiscites in the British Cameroons", ["a new railway", "a world war", "an exhibition"], "The vote was set for 1961."),
     ("Independence of the whole of Cameroon in one day in 1960 is", "incorrect", ["correct", "proved by UN", "approved by Britain"], "Only French Cameroun became independent then.")],
    ["Teacher to verify: dates (1 January 1960; 1 October 1960; the 1959 UN decision) and the wording of the plebiscite options; avoid taking sides in the debate on the options."], tag="Form 4 history independence 1960")

L = LS(c, "plebiscite-reunification", "The plebiscites of 1961 and reunification",
    ["Describe the plebiscites of February 1961", "State the results in outline", "Describe the Foumban Conference and the reunification of 1 October 1961", "Explain the importance of 11 February and 1 October"],
    [("text", "On **11 February 1961** the plebiscites were held. In the **Southern Cameroons** a majority voted to **join the Republic of Cameroon**. In the **Northern Cameroons** a majority voted to **join Nigeria**. The Northern Cameroons became part of Nigeria on **1 June 1961**. For this reason the Southern Cameroons and the Republic of Cameroon were the two units that joined together."),
     ("key", "retenir", "The Foumban Conference", "In **July 1961** at **Foumban**, leaders of the Southern Cameroons and the Republic of Cameroon met to discuss the **constitution** for the new union. The discussion led to a **federal constitution** with two federated states, East and West Cameroon, a federal government and a federal president. People still debate the terms of the Foumban talks and later changes."),
     ("key", "retenir", "1 October 1961", "On **1 October 1961** the Southern Cameroons ended British trusteeship and joined the Republic of Cameroon to form the **Federal Republic of Cameroon**. This is called **reunification**, as the two parts of the old German Kamerun were joined again. The Southern Cameroons became **West Cameroon** with Buea as capital; the Republic of Cameroon became **East Cameroon**. Yaoundé was the federal capital."),
     ("text", "**Reunification** did not remove all differences: the two parts had different languages, laws, school systems, money and administrative habits. The new state adopted **English and French** as official languages and tried to build a common identity. National days celebrated include **11 February** (Youth Day, from the plebiscites) and **20 May** (National Day, linked to the 1972 referendum), as well as **1 October** in memory of reunification."),
     ("key", "pieges", "Common mistakes", "- The Northern Cameroons voted to join Nigeria; do not say the entire British Cameroons joined Cameroon.\n- Reunification (1 October 1961) is different from independence (1 January 1960).\n- The Foumban Conference (July 1961) took place before the union on 1 October.")],
    [("Example 1: results", "What was the result of the plebiscite in the Southern Cameroons and in the Northern Cameroons?", ["Southern Cameroons: a majority for joining the Republic of Cameroon.", "Northern Cameroons: a majority for joining Nigeria.", "Both votes took place on 11 February 1961."], "South: join Cameroon; North: join Nigeria."),
     ("Example 2: the new state", "Name the two states of the Federal Republic of Cameroon in 1961.", ["The Southern Cameroons became West Cameroon.", "The Republic of Cameroon became East Cameroon.", "Together they formed the Federal Republic."], "West Cameroon and East Cameroon.")],
    F(flow("Road to reunification", ["11 Feb 1961|Plebiscites", "1 Jun 1961|North: Nigeria", "July 1961|Foumban", "1 Oct 1961|reunification"], per_row=4), "From plebiscite to reunification", "Four boxes showing the plebiscite, the Northern Cameroons joining Nigeria, the Foumban talks and reunification."),
    [MCQ("The plebiscites in the British Cameroons were held on", "11 February 1961", ["1 January 1960", "20 May 1972", "1 October 1960"], "That is the date."),
     TF("The Northern Cameroons voted to join Nigeria.", True, "A majority chose Nigeria."),
     MATCH("Match each date with its event.", [("11 February 1961", "Plebiscites"), ("July 1961", "Foumban Conference"), ("1 October 1961", "Reunification of Southern Cameroons and Cameroon")], "Dates in 1961."),
     MCQ("After 1 October 1961 the capital of West Cameroon was", "Buea", ["Foumban", "Garoua", "Douala"], "Buea was the West Cameroon capital.", tier="approfondissement"),
     OPEN("Explain the importance of the Foumban Conference of 1961.", "Leaders from the two sides met to discuss the constitution of the union; it led to a federal constitution creating East and West Cameroon, so it laid the legal basis for reunification and showed the need to respect two systems.", "1 mark: leaders met; 1 mark: constitution; 1 mark: federal structure.", points=3, tier="approfondissement"),
     PROB("A student writes a note on the events of 1961.",
          [("mcq", "The two parts that formed the Federal Republic were", "the Southern Cameroons and the Republic of Cameroon", ["Nigeria and Chad", "Gabon and Congo", "Ghana and Togo"], "They joined on 1 October 1961.", {}),
           ("num", "In which year did the Federal Republic come into being?", 1961, "On 1 October 1961.", 0, None, {}),
           ("tf", "Youth Day is celebrated on 11 February.", True, "It marks the plebiscite date.", {}),
           ("open", "State two differences that remained between the two parts after reunification.", "Languages (English and French), legal systems, school systems, currencies and administrative traditions.", "1 mark per difference.", {})])],
    [("The Northern Cameroons joined Nigeria on", "1 June 1961", ["1 October 1961", "1 January 1960", "11 February 1961"], "It joined in June 1961."),
     ("The Foumban Conference was held in", "July 1961", ["January 1960", "December 1972", "October 1960"], "It discussed the federal constitution."),
     ("West Cameroon was the former", "Southern Cameroons", ["Northern Cameroons", "Republic of Cameroon", "Nigeria"], "It joined the Republic of Cameroon."),
     ("The federal capital in 1961 was", "Yaoundé", ["Buea", "Limbe", "Bamenda"], "The federal government was in Yaoundé."),
     ("Reunification means", "joining together again the two parts of old Kamerun", ["dividing Cameroon", "ending the UN", "selling land"], "The two parts were once Kamerun.")],
    ["Teacher to verify: dates (11 February 1961; Northern Cameroons joining Nigeria on 1 June 1961; Foumban in July 1961; 1 October 1961), the names of the two states, the origin of national days; vote counts and the debate about the terms of union are intentionally not given."], tag="Form 4 history 1961")

L = LS(c, "federation-unitary", "From the Federal Republic to the unitary state",
    ["Describe the federal system of 1961–1972", "Explain the referendum of 20 May 1972", "Describe the names of the state: Federal Republic, United Republic and Republic of Cameroon", "Describe the Constitution of 1996 in outline"],
    [("text", "From **1961 to 1972** Cameroon was a **federation** of two states: **East Cameroon** and **West Cameroon**, each with its own government and assembly, under a federal president and federal government. The aim was to protect the identities of the two parts (such as their languages and legal traditions) while building one nation. Federal institutions were in Yaoundé and West Cameroon's in Buea."),
     ("key", "retenir", "The 1972 referendum", "On **20 May 1972** a **referendum** was held, in which the people voted on a new constitution creating a **unitary state**: the **United Republic of Cameroon**. The federal structure ended and the two states were merged into one country with one government. Supporters said it would strengthen unity and save costs; others have criticised the loss of federal safeguards. **20 May** became the **National Day**."),
     ("text", "In **1984** the country's name was changed to the **Republic of Cameroon**, the name it had in 1960. A new **Constitution** was adopted in **1996** and later amended. It describes Cameroon as a **decentralised unitary state**, with English and French as official languages, and creates **regions**, a **Senate** and other institutions. The decentralisation process aims to give local councils and regions more responsibility."),
     ("key", "retenir", "Changes in the name and form of the state", "- 1960: Republic of Cameroon (French Cameroun).\n- 1961: Federal Republic of Cameroon.\n- 1972: United Republic of Cameroon.\n- 1984: Republic of Cameroon.\nThe 1996 Constitution is the current basic law, with later amendments."),
     ("key", "attention", "Be careful", "- This topic is sensitive: present facts and the different views fairly and with respect.\n- A federation has two or more states sharing power; a unitary state has one central government with possibly local units.\n- Always give dates and names correctly.")],
    [("Example 1: federation or unitary?", "Which describes Cameroon in 1965 and which in 1975?", ["In 1965 there were two federated states.", "In 1975 there was a unitary state after the 1972 referendum.", "So 1965 federation, 1975 unitary state."], "1965: federation; 1975: unitary state."),
     ("Example 2: why 20 May?", "Why is 20 May celebrated as National Day?", ["It is the date of the 1972 referendum.", "The referendum created the unitary United Republic of Cameroon.", "So the day celebrates the unitary state."], "It commemorates the 1972 referendum creating the unitary state.")],
    F(tlfig(1959, 1997, [(1960, "1960"), (1961, "1961"), (1972, "1972"), (1984, "1984"), (1996, "1996")], h=210), "Names and forms of the state", "A timeline from 1959 to 1997 with 1960, 1961, 1972, 1984 and 1996."),
    [MCQ("In Form 4 history, the referendum creating a unitary state was held on", "20 May 1972", ["11 February 1961", "1 October 1961", "1 January 1960"], "That is the date."),
     TF("From 1961 to 1972 Cameroon was a federation of East and West Cameroon.", True, "Two federated states."),
     MATCH("Match each date with the name of the state.", [("1961", "Federal Republic of Cameroon"), ("1972", "United Republic of Cameroon"), ("1984", "Republic of Cameroon")], "Changes of name."),
     MCQ("The Constitution of 1996 describes Cameroon as", "a decentralised unitary state", ["a monarchy", "a confederation of three states", "a colony"], "That is the standard description.", tier="approfondissement"),
     OPEN("Explain, neutrally, why some people supported the change to a unitary state in 1972 and some criticised it.", "Supporters said a single state would strengthen national unity and reduce costs of two governments; critics felt that the federal safeguards for the English-speaking and French-speaking traditions were lost and wanted their identities better protected.", "1 mark each for supporters' view, critics' view and fair language.", points=3, tier="approfondissement"),
     PROB("A class compares the state in 1965 and in 2000.",
          [("mcq", "In 1965 the territory was", "a federation", ["a protectorate", "a mandate", "a kingdom"], "East and West Cameroon.", {}),
           ("num", "In which year was the Constitution described as 'decentralised unitary state' adopted?", 1996, "The Constitution dates from 1996.", 0, None, {}),
           ("tf", "The unitary state was created by a referendum.", True, "On 20 May 1972.", {}),
           ("open", "State two features of the 1996 Constitution.", "It describes a decentralised unitary state, with English and French as official languages, and provides for regions, a Senate and a National Assembly.", "1 mark per feature.", {})])],
    [("A federation consists of", "two or more states under a federal government", ["no government", "only one town", "a school"], "Power is shared."),
     ("The National Day of Cameroon is", "20 May", ["11 February", "1 October", "1 January"], "It is linked to the 1972 referendum."),
     ("The 1984 change was to the", "name of the country", ["capital city", "flag colours only", "anthem only"], "It became the Republic of Cameroon."),
     ("A referendum such as the one of 1972 is", "a vote by the people on a proposal", ["a trial in court", "a school test", "a trade fair"], "It was used in 1972."),
     ("The official languages of Cameroon are", "English and French", ["Duala and Bamum", "Arabic and Spanish", "German and Latin"], "Both have equal status.")],
    ["Teacher to verify all dates (20 May 1972; the 1984 renaming; the 1996 Constitution) and the neutral phrasing of the debate about federation; the lesson does not name any person and takes no side."], tag="Form 4 history federation")

# ------------------------------------------------------------ CH3 world
c = p.chapter("world", "International organisations and the Cold War")
L = LS(c, "international-orgs", "International and regional organisations",
    ["Describe the UN and its main organs", "Describe the OAU and the African Union", "Describe the Commonwealth and La Francophonie", "Explain Cameroon's membership and regional groupings"],
    [("text", "Countries cooperate through **international organisations**. The **United Nations (UN)**, founded in **1945**, aims to keep peace, protect human rights and promote development. Its main organs include the **General Assembly** (all members), the **Security Council** (responsible for peace and security), the **International Court of Justice**, the **Secretariat** and agencies such as **UNESCO**, **WHO** and **UNICEF**. Cameroon joined the UN in 1960."),
     ("key", "retenir", "OAU and AU", "The **Organisation of African Unity (OAU)** was founded in **May 1963** at **Addis Ababa**. Its aims were African unity, defence of independence, end of colonialism and apartheid, and cooperation. The **African Union (AU)** replaced it in **2002**, with broader aims, such as peace, democracy, economic integration and human rights, and a Peace and Security Council."),
     ("key", "retenir", "Commonwealth and Francophonie", "The **Commonwealth** is an association of countries, most of them former British colonies, which cooperate in education, trade and democracy. The **Francophonie** is a similar association of countries that share the French language and cultural ties. **Cameroon** is a member of both, which reflects its two colonial histories (it joined the Commonwealth in the 1990s)."),
     ("text", "**Regional groupings** in Africa aim to promote trade and cooperation: **CEMAC** in Central Africa (Cameroon, Chad, the Central African Republic, Congo, Equatorial Guinea and Gabon) and **ECOWAS** in West Africa. Benefits include larger markets, free movement of goods and shared projects such as roads. Difficulties include weak infrastructure, similar economies, political disputes and shortages of money."),
     ("key", "attention", "Be careful", "- The AU replaced the OAU in 2002; they are not the same organisation, but the AU grew out of the OAU.\n- Membership of the Commonwealth does not mean being ruled by Britain; members are independent states.\n- Regional groupings are not the same as the UN: they cover a smaller area with specific aims.")],
    [("Example 1: organs of the UN", "Which UN organ includes all members and which is responsible for peace and security?", ["The General Assembly includes all members.", "The Security Council deals with peace and security.", "So Assembly and Council."], "General Assembly; Security Council."),
     ("Example 2: OAU to AU", "Name the city where the OAU was founded and the year the AU replaced it.", ["The OAU was founded in Addis Ababa in 1963.", "The AU replaced it in 2002.", "So Addis Ababa and 2002."], "Addis Ababa; 2002.")],
    F(tlfig(1944, 2004, [(1945, "1945"), (1963, "1963"), (2002, "2002")], h=200), "International organisations", "A timeline from 1944 to 2004 with the UN in 1945, the OAU in 1963 and the AU in 2002."),
    [MCQ("The Organisation of African Unity was founded in", "1963", ["1884", "1914", "2020"], "It was founded in May 1963."),
     TF("The African Union replaced the OAU in 2002.", True, "The AU succeeded the OAU."),
     MATCH("Match each organisation with its main aim.", [("UN", "Peace, rights and development worldwide"), ("AU", "African unity, peace and integration"), ("CEMAC", "Economic cooperation in Central Africa")], "International and regional bodies."),
     MCQ("The Commonwealth is mainly an association of", "former British colonies and others", ["only French-speaking states", "only Asian countries", "only European states"], "Many members were British colonies.", tier="approfondissement"),
     OPEN("Explain two benefits and two difficulties of regional groupings such as CEMAC.", "Benefits: bigger market and easier trade, joint projects like roads. Difficulties: poor transport links, similar products so little trade among members, political disputes and lack of funds.", "1 mark per correct point.", points=4, tier="approfondissement"),
     PROB("A student prepares a chart of organisations.",
          [("mcq", "The UN was founded in", "1945", ["1914", "1960", "1990"], "After the Second World War.", {}),
           ("tf", "UNESCO and WHO are agencies connected with the UN.", True, "They are specialised agencies.", {}),
           ("mcq", "Which is a regional grouping of Central Africa?", "CEMAC", ["NATO", "OPEC", "EU"], "CEMAC groups Central African states.", {}),
           ("open", "Give two roles of the African Union.", "Promoting peace and security, democracy and human rights, economic integration and cooperation between African states.", "1 mark per role.", {})])],
    [("The headquarters of the African Union is in", "Addis Ababa", ["Yaoundé", "London", "Paris"], "It is the AU's seat as it was for the OAU."),
     ("The organ of the UN responsible for peace and security is the", "Security Council", ["Trusteeship Council", "Secretariat only", "ILO"], "It can authorise peace operations."),
     ("Cameroon is a member of both the Commonwealth and the", "Francophonie", ["Warsaw Pact", "NATO", "Arab League"], "It has English and French heritage."),
     ("CEMAC stands for", "Central African Economic and Monetary Community", ["Canadian Economic Market", "Common East Market of Africa", "Central Euro Market"], "It is the Central African grouping."),
     ("UNESCO deals mainly with", "education, science and culture", ["wars", "banking", "airlines"], "It is a UN agency.")],
    ["Teacher to verify: UN founding (1945) and Cameroon's UN admission (1960), the OAU date (May 1963, Addis Ababa), the AU's replacement of the OAU (2002), Cameroon's Commonwealth membership (1990s) and the CEMAC membership list."], tag="Form 4 history organisations")

L = LS(c, "cold-war", "The Cold War in outline and its effect on Africa",
    ["Define the Cold War and name the two blocs", "Describe some main events", "Explain how the Cold War affected Africa", "Describe the end of the Cold War"],
    [("text", "The **Cold War** was a long rivalry after the Second World War between two superpowers, the **United States** and the **Soviet Union (USSR)**, and their allies. It was called 'cold' because the two sides never fought each other directly in a full war, but competed through **arms races**, propaganda, spying, economic aid and **proxy wars** in other countries. It lasted from about **1945 to 1991**."),
     ("key", "retenir", "Two blocs", "The **Western bloc** led by the USA was **capitalist and democratic** and formed **NATO** in 1949. The **Eastern bloc** led by the USSR was **communist** and formed the **Warsaw Pact** in 1955. Germany was divided, and Berlin too; the **Berlin Wall** was built in 1961 and fell in **1989**. The **Cuban Missile Crisis** of 1962 brought the world close to nuclear war."),
     ("text", "**Effects on Africa.** Both blocs tried to win friends among the new African states. They gave **aid, weapons and advisers**, and some wars in Africa (such as in Angola) became linked to the rivalry. Some African states chose one side, others followed **non-alignment**, refusing to join either bloc. African leaders also used the rivalry to get aid. The UN and the OAU worked for peace and decolonisation."),
     ("key", "retenir", "The end", "In **1989–1991** communist governments in eastern Europe collapsed, the Berlin Wall fell and in **December 1991** the **Soviet Union** broke up. The Cold War ended. In Africa the end brought new demands for **multiparty democracy** and changes in aid, although also new conflicts and economic challenges."),
     ("key", "pieges", "Common mistakes", "- 'Cold' does not mean a climate: it means no direct large-scale war between the superpowers.\n- Non-alignment is not neutrality in all matters; it means not joining a military bloc.\n- Keep to the dates: NATO 1949, Berlin Wall 1961, Cuban crisis 1962, USSR ends 1991.")],
    [("Example 1: blocs", "Name the leaders of the two blocs and an organisation for each.", ["The USA led the Western bloc, which formed NATO.", "The USSR led the Eastern bloc, which formed the Warsaw Pact.", "So USA–NATO and USSR–Warsaw Pact."], "USA with NATO; USSR with the Warsaw Pact."),
     ("Example 2: effect on Africa", "Give one way in which the Cold War affected an African state.", ["Both blocs offered aid and weapons.", "Some conflicts became linked to the rivalry.", "So an African state might receive aid or be drawn into a proxy war."], "Aid and weapons; involvement in proxy wars.")],
    F(cols("The two blocs", [("West (USA)", ["NATO 1949", "Capitalist", "Democratic"]), ("East (USSR)", ["Warsaw Pact 1955", "Communist", "One-party rule"])], w=440), "Cold War blocs", "Two columns summarising the Western and Eastern blocs."),
    [MCQ("In Form 4 history, the Cold War was a rivalry between", "the USA and the USSR", ["Britain and France", "Cameroon and Nigeria", "Germany and Japan only"], "These were the two superpowers."),
     TF("The Berlin Wall fell in 1989.", True, "It marked the coming end of the Cold War."),
     MATCH("Match each event with its date.", [("NATO founded", "1949"), ("Cuban Missile Crisis", "1962"), ("Break-up of the USSR", "1991")], "Cold War dates."),
     MCQ("Non-alignment means", "refusing to join either bloc", ["joining both blocs", "ending the UN", "ruling a colony"], "Many new states followed it.", tier="approfondissement"),
     OPEN("Explain two effects of the Cold War on Africa.", "Both blocs gave aid, weapons and advisers to African governments or movements, which prolonged some conflicts; others followed non-alignment and used the rivalry to win aid, and the end of the Cold War brought pressure for multiparty democracy.", "1 mark per effect with explanation.", points=3, tier="approfondissement"),
     PROB("A student writes about the end of the Cold War.",
          [("num", "In which year did the Soviet Union break up?", 1991, "The USSR was dissolved in December 1991.", 0, None, {}),
           ("mcq", "The Western military alliance formed in 1949 was", "NATO", ["the Warsaw Pact", "the OAU", "the AU"], "NATO was a Western alliance.", {}),
           ("tf", "The superpowers fought each other directly in a major war.", False, "That is why it was 'cold'.", {}),
           ("open", "Give two ways the superpowers competed without direct war.", "Arms races, propaganda, spying, space race, economic aid and support for allies in other countries' wars.", "1 mark per way.", {})])],
    [("The Warsaw Pact was founded in", "1955", ["1914", "1960", "1991"], "It was the Eastern military alliance."),
     ("A proxy war is a war in which", "superpowers support opposing sides in another country", ["superpowers fight directly", "no one fights", "only the UN fights"], "They fought through others."),
     ("NATO was led by the", "United States", ["Soviet Union", "Cameroon", "Egypt"], "The USA led NATO."),
     ("The Soviet Union ended in", "1991", ["1945", "1961", "1972"], "It broke into separate states."),
     ("Non-aligned countries were", "not part of either bloc", ["members of NATO only", "members of the Warsaw Pact only", "colonies of the USA"], "They kept independence of the blocs.")],
    ["Teacher to verify: dates (NATO 1949, Warsaw Pact 1955, Berlin Wall 1961 and 1989, Cuban crisis 1962, USSR dissolved in December 1991) and the examples of African conflicts; the lesson uses neutral wording about both blocs."], tag="Form 4 history Cold War")

